Blocking issues (1–6)
1. API key. Every route except /health now needs an X-API-Key header. If API_KEY isn't set on the server, it refuses everything. Both workflows send the key, read from the n8n variable LINKEDIN_AGENT_API_KEY.
2. Human-review routing. Items that ask for a call now reach n8n as action=human_review. The normal send path also checks for call requests before replying. The keyword match no longer fires on "rappelle" or "s'appelle".
3. Recipient check. The "we clicked from the right profile" fallback is gone, and the page-wide text match is replaced by a check of the thread header. Reply-by-name only accepts a thread whose name matches the full requested name, and the first-name-only search is removed.
4. Safety hold. A checkpoint, login wall, CAPTCHA frame, restriction or weekly-limit banner, or HTTP 429 now freezes every action. The hold persists until POST /safety-hold/clear.
5. Initialization. /detect-connections returns 409 until /initialize-connections has run once, and initialize now covers the top 100. I also found that initialized contacts were still being treated as "to contact", so they would have been messaged anyway. That's fixed.
6. State. A corrupt state file is restored from a backup copy, or the service stops, instead of silently starting from empty. Writes are now under the lock.

Content fixes (7–9)
- The false "J'ai liké et partagé votre post" line is gone. There's a MENTION_SHARED_POST=false switch in the n8n code for contacts the consultant really did like or share for.
- The persona can no longer deny being an AI, and the invented testimonial and personal anecdotes are removed. Huntzen Jobs is now presented as Huntzen's own service.
- The AI is told to ignore instructions inside candidate messages.
- The AI now answers NO_REPLY or HUMAN_REVIEW when appropriate, and n8n drops replies that contain links or run too long.
- There are 4 first-message variants, picked by a hash of the profile URL.

Quality fixes (10–16)
- Huntzen Jobs is detected in any spelling, so the J+14 follow-up isn't lost.
- Thank-you messages and threads at the reply cap (4 per contact) are skipped.
- Follow-ups skip contacts known only by thread URL.
- The n8n reply loop now sends one reply at a time with a pause, instead of firing them in a burst.
- SQL is parameterised, and Sent is only recorded when a message was actually sent.
- Auto-accepting invitations is off by default.
- Debug screenshots are off by default and rotated when on.

Process (17–19)
- Ramp-up: messages start at 10 per day and add 10 each week, replies start at 20 and add 20, and invitations start at 10 and add 5.
- Pacing: the server enforces at least 30 s between sends.
- Kill switch: POST /pause and POST /resume. The daily report shows holds and pauses.
- Client sign-off: RISK_ACCEPTANCE.md (in French) is ready for the client to sign.

Beyond your list
- The n8n Wait node had no unit, so it probably waited 20 hours per contact. It's now 40–120 s.
- I removed the mouse-movement and random-pause "human" simulation, which contradicted the "no evasion" claim.
- Both cron triggers had no interval set. I set the inbox to 30 minutes and the prospecting one to 60. Change them if the client wants other values.
- A failed first-message send no longer stops the rest of the batch.
- The first-message timeout is now 180 s, because of the send gap.
- The activity window now ends at 19:00 instead of 19:59.

Before going live
- Set API_KEY on the server and LINKEDIN_AGENT_API_KEY in n8n. If n8n blocks $env, use an n8n credential instead.
- Run /initialize-connections once per account.
- The n8n Postgres parameter syntax and node connections need a check after import.
- Serve the API over HTTPS or a private network. The workflows still call http://46.62.194.135:5000.
- I couldn't create the .env.example file because your permission settings block it. The main settings to define are API_KEY, HOST (127.0.0.1 if n8n is on the same machine), STATE_FILE, ACCOUNT_LABEL, the daily limits and the RAMP_* variables. I can put them in a differently named file or a doc if you'd like.


